\title{Lower bounds from short cycles}
\author{Human Compression project: a component of the F012 recovery}
\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We give the precise elementary and inherited arguments for eighty-six
lower-bound entries in Tables 1--4 of Goedgebeur and Jooken's fixed
June 2024 manuscript. A ledger identifies every parameter entry, its
source bound and its proof route. The other fifty-six entries remain
outside this component. Neither a positive example nor a failed finite
search is used to infer nonexistence.
\end{abstract}

\section{The common counting argument}
An $\egr(n,k,g,\lambda)$ is a finite connected simple $k$-regular graph
of girth $g$ with exactly $\lambda$ unoriented girth cycles through
each edge. We use $n(k,g,\lambda)=\infty$ only when no such graph exists.
The source sometimes prints infinity as an upper bound when no example
is known. That notation is not a nonexistence assertion.

Counting incident edge--cycle pairs at a vertex, and then in the whole
graph, gives
\[
 2\mid k\lambda,\qquad 2\mid nk,\qquad 2g\mid nk\lambda.       \tag{1}
\]
Indeed, a cycle through a vertex uses two incident edges; the total
number of girth cycles is $nk\lambda/(2g)$; and $nk=2|E|$.
These are the source's attributed elementary properties of
Jajcay, Kiss and Miklavi\v c.

Put
\[
 M(k,2s+1)=1+k\sum_{j=0}^{s-1}(k-1)^j,
 \qquad M(k,2s)=2\sum_{j=0}^{s-1}(k-1)^j.
\]
We acquire the complete proof of Lemma 1, labelled \texttt{bounds}, in
the accepted L025 manuscript \emph{Cycle counts in biaffine and Suzuki
incidence graphs}. It proves the Drglin--Filipovski--Jajcay--Raiman bounds
\[
 n\ge B(k,g,\lambda):=
 \begin{cases}
 M(k,2s+1)+(k-1)^s-\lambda,&g=2s+1,\\[2pt]
 M(k,2s)+\lceil 2((k-1)^s-\lambda)/k\rceil,&g=2s.
 \end{cases}                                                     \tag{2}
\]
This is an acquisition of the actual tree-counting proof, not an
assumption that the desired F012 table is correct. The declared
dependency consists only of that lemma. In particular, we do not use
the later L025 clause $n(4,6,18)\ge32$, whose justification cites F012.

For clarity, the count also supplies an equality fact used below.
At odd girth $2s+1$, the ball of radius $s$ about a vertex is a tree
apart from edges between its outermost vertices. It has $M(k,2s+1)$
vertices. The extra edges are in bijection with girth cycles through
the root, so there are $k\lambda/2$ of them. After these internal
edges are counted, exactly $k((k-1)^s-\lambda)$ edges leave the ball.
Consequently, if $\lambda=(k-1)^s$, connectedness makes this ball the
whole graph.

For each triple in the source tables, we round (2) upward to the first
integer satisfying the last two conditions of (1). All table triples
already satisfy the first condition. The resulting bound reaches the
printed lower bound for sixty-three entries. The exact substitutions,
including the integer before rounding, are in \texttt{row\_ledger.json}.

\section{Two local estimates}
\begin{lemma}\label{local}
For girth three and positive $\lambda$, one has
\[
 n\ge3(k-\lambda).
\]
For girth four, one has
\[
 n\ge k^2+1-\frac{k\lambda}{2}.                            \tag{3}
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Choose a triangle $uvw$. The three open neighborhoods have size $k$,
and each pair intersects in exactly $\lambda$ vertices, since common
neighbors of the endpoints of an edge are precisely its triangles.
Inclusion--exclusion therefore gives
\[
 |N(u)\cup N(v)\cup N(w)|
 =3k-3\lambda+|N(u)\cap N(v)\cap N(w)|\ge3(k-\lambda).
\]
The union is a subset of the vertex set. This proves the first bound,
which is the familiar triangle-neighborhood estimate used in the
earlier edge-regular literature.

For the second bound, fix a vertex $v$ and let $S$ be the vertices at
distance two from $v$. For $w\in S$ write $m_w=|N(w)\cap N(v)|$.
There are no triangles, so all $k(k-1)$ paths of length two starting
at $v$ and not returning to $v$ end in $S$. Thus
\[
 \sum_{w\in S}m_w=k(k-1).
\]
A choice of two common neighbors of $v$ and $w$ gives exactly one
quadrangle through $v$, and each such quadrangle has a unique vertex
opposite $v$. Hence
\[
 \sum_{w\in S}\binom{m_w}{2}=\frac{k\lambda}{2}.
\]
Every $m_w$ is positive, and $m_w-1\le\binom{m_w}{2}$. Subtracting
gives $|S|\ge k(k-1)-k\lambda/2$. The disjoint sets $\{v\}$,
$N(v)$ and $S$ prove (3).
\end{proof}

After (1) is imposed, Lemma~\ref{local} supplies the following nine
entries not reached by (2) alone:
\[
\begin{array}{ccc|rr}
k&g&\lambda&\text{source lower bound}&\text{bound here}\\\hline
4&4&2&13&13\\
5&4&2&24&24\\
6&3&1&15&15\\
6&3&2&12&12\\
6&4&1&32&36\\
6&4&2&30&32\\
6&4&3&28&28\\
6&4&4&25&25\\
6&4&5&24&24
\end{array}
\]
For example, (3) gives $n\ge34$ when $(k,g,\lambda)=(6,4,1)$;
the divisibility $8\mid6n$ requires $4\mid n$, hence $n\ge36$.
The two stronger values in this table are recorded as consequences
of the stated estimate. We make no priority claim for the estimate
or for these numerical improvements.

\section{The thirteen nonexistence entries}
\begin{proposition}\label{odd}
There are no graphs with triples
\[
 (3,7,8),\quad(4,5,9),\quad(5,5,16),\quad(6,5,25).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
In every case $\lambda=(k-1)^{(g-1)/2}$. The saturated-ball argument
after (2) gives a Moore graph: every vertex has all vertices within
distance $(g-1)/2$, with a unique shortest path to each of them.
We give the needed small-degree exclusions directly.

First let $g=5$ and $k\in\{4,5,6\}$. There are $k^2+1$ vertices.
Let $A$ be its adjacency matrix and $J$ the all-one matrix. Adjacent
vertices have no common neighbor, whereas distinct nonadjacent
vertices have exactly one. Therefore
\[
 A^2+A-(k-1)I=J.                                          \tag{4}
\]
On the subspace perpendicular to the all-one vector, every eigenvalue
is a root of $f(x)=x^2+x-(k-1)$. Its discriminant is respectively
$13$, $17$ or $21$, so $f$ is irreducible over the rationals. The
characteristic polynomial of the integer matrix $A$ is consequently
$(x-k)f(x)^a$: the principal eigenvalue $k$ is simple by connectedness,
and the conjugate roots have the same multiplicity. Its degree gives
$2a=k^2$, while $\operatorname{tr}A=0$ gives $a=k$. These equations
would force $k=2$, a contradiction.

For $k=3$ and $g=7$, there are twenty-two vertices and diameter three.
Let $A_i$ be the distance-$i$ matrix. The unique shortest paths give
$A_2=A^2-3I$ and $AA_2=A_3+2A$. To check the latter identity, count
neighbors of $x$ at distance two from $y$. There are two if $x,y$ are
adjacent, one if their distance is three, and none if their distance
is zero or two; a second path in the last two cases would create a
cycle of length at most six. Thus
\[
 J=I+A+A_2+A_3=A^3+A^2-4A-2I.                            \tag{5}
\]
The cubic $x^3+x^2-4x-2$ has no rational root: its possible integral
roots $1,-1,2,-2$ give respectively $-4,2,2,2$. It is irreducible.
As above, the characteristic polynomial must be
$(x-3)(x^3+x^2-4x-2)^7$. Its roots sum to $3-7=-4$, contrary to
the zero diagonal of $A$.
\end{proof}
These are precisely the four odd-girth maximal-count exclusions
attributed to Jajcay, Kiss and Miklavi\v c in the source tables.
The proof here uses only the displayed matrix identities and
elementary irreducibility, not a general classification of Moore graphs.

For the even-girth entries we retain an external theorem explicitly.
Theorem 2.4 of Kiss, Miklavi\v c and Sz\H onyi states that a
girth-regular graph of degree $k$ and girth $2d$, whose largest
signature entry is $(k-1)^d-\varepsilon$ with
$0\le\varepsilon<k-1$, has $\varepsilon=0$.
A signature lists the girth-cycle counts of the incident edges at
each vertex, in increasing order. In an edge-girth-regular graph all
its entries are $\lambda$, so the theorem applies without any
transitivity assumption. In particular
\[
 0<(k-1)^{g/2}-\lambda<k-1
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad n(k,g,\lambda)=\infty.           \tag{6}
\]
It gives the nine entries
\[
 (4,4,7),(4,4,8),(4,6,25),(4,6,26),(5,4,14),
 (6,4,21),(6,4,22),(6,4,23),(6,4,24).
\]
This is the same prior theorem used by the source. Its complete proof
is not reproduced or claimed as a new result here. The acquisition
record identifies the author preprint, the exact theorem and the
limits of the available source inspection.

\section{One retained finite comparison and the row audit}
For $(k,g,\lambda)=(5,5,12)$, (2) gives $n\ge30$ and odd degree
makes $n$ even. We acquire the independent lower-bound argument in
the L025 proposition labelled \texttt{small}. It retains the complete
classification of the four $(5,5)$-cages of order thirty and checks
their literal graphs. The edge-count histograms, in its retained
order, are
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
 &\lambda_e=12&\lambda_e=13&\lambda_e=14&\lambda_e=16\\\hline
1&45&0&30&0\\
2&50&20&5&0\\
3&60&0&15&0\\
4&24&48&0&3
\end{array}
\]
Every row has an edge outside the column $12$. Thus none is an
$\egr(30,5,5,12)$, and $n(5,5,12)\ge32$. The completeness premise
remains borrowed; the accepted source data, two finite reports and
distinct review are pinned. This particular proposition depends on
the tree bound and the four cages, not on F012. No other L025 result
is imported by this argument.

The ledger has one entry for each of the 142 parameter triples in
Tables 1--4, expanding a printed range into its individual values.
Its eighty-six completed entries comprise sixty-three uses of (2),
nine uses of Lemma~\ref{local}, four uses of Proposition~\ref{odd},
nine uses of (6), and the single four-cage comparison. Each entry
records the printed row, physical page, original attribution and
the exact result proved here. The checker recomputes all integer
substitutions and verifies the partition of the ledger.

The other fifty-six entries are marked unresolved by this component.
Among them, $n(4,4,1)\ge18$ and $n(4,4,3)\ge14$ are attributed by
the source to Drglin and coauthors, but their original proofs or
complete classifications have not been acquired here. Their
presence in the F012 table is not itself used as a proof. The
separate accepted girth-nine component and the root's other
recoveries may cover some of the remaining entries; this ledger
does not pre-empt that combined audit. It makes no assertion about
uniqueness, the number of extremal graphs, Table 5, or a complete
recovery of F012.

